1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,280
To fast is to hunger for more of God.

2
00:00:16,280 --> 00:00:23,880
We fast to offer our whole selves, including our whole body, to God.

3
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:28,600
To grow in holiness.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,220
To amplify our prayers.

5
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:38,540
And to stand with the poor.

6
00:00:38,540 --> 00:00:46,900
Fasting is one of the most essential and powerful of all the practices of Jesus.

7
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:53,740
It's an ancient discipline whose time has come.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:13,720
Lord singing together the chorus of here I am to worship and then that comes on.

9
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:19,660
I was waiting for anybody or everybody to stand up and walk out after that.

10
00:01:19,660 --> 00:01:22,180
I remember it reminded me of a time in college.

11
00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:24,600
My freshman year of college I didn't have a car.

12
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:29,460
And so there was a buddy of mine named Mike who would give me a ride to church.

13
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:32,700
He was a pretty big guy, red beard.

14
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:36,200
He was the comedian of the entire, you know, dorm room.

15
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:39,160
And we went to church one Sunday.

16
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:40,240
We were walking down the aisle.

17
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:42,080
And they had the bulletins that lay down on the chair.

18
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:44,920
Granted, he's a bigger dude.

19
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:47,200
And he picks up the bulletin.

20
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:50,220
And the front cover of the bulletin says just one word, just gluttony.

21
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:52,500
He stops.

22
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:53,460
He looks at the bulletin.

23
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:54,040
He looks at me.

24
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:56,780
And he goes, well, I think we've been here long enough.

25
00:01:58,520 --> 00:01:59,780
And he turns and walks away.

26
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:00,640
And he was my ride.

27
00:02:00,640 --> 00:02:01,960
So there it goes.

28
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:04,560
Anyway, that was a joke.

29
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:05,360
That was not in the notes.

30
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:05,900
That was free.

31
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:06,300
You're welcome.

32
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:09,960
If we haven't had the privilege to meet, did I introduce myself yet?

33
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:10,820
Okay.

34
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:11,560
My name is Ryan.

35
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:13,460
I have the privilege of being one of the pastors here.

36
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:15,960
And I'm excited that you have joined us.

37
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:21,220
At the beginning of this year, we launched a brand new series called Practicing the Way.

38
00:02:21,220 --> 00:02:25,660
It's built around nine practices from the life and teachings of Jesus.

39
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:28,760
And it really is a practice-based discipleship.

40
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:34,260
The church in the West tends to have a more intellectually driven discipleship process.

41
00:02:34,260 --> 00:02:40,560
What we think about tends to be the thing that we look to to transform us.

42
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:48,040
And this is a radical invitation to not just invest our minds in our discipleship, but our whole selves.

43
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:55,100
The number one commandment when Jesus is asked is to love the Lord your God with all of your heart, your soul, your mind, and your strength.

44
00:02:55,480 --> 00:03:04,680
So this series is really aimed at engaging all of ourselves and offering ourselves to God so that he might transform us into the image of Jesus.

45
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:11,060
Let us not forget, church, that the purpose of following Jesus is that we might become like Jesus.

46
00:03:11,060 --> 00:03:18,300
To introduce the rest of the world to Jesus, we believe that he is the ultimate example of what true humanity looks like.

47
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:21,520
That because he created everything, he knows how everything works.

48
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:27,040
And if we'll submit our lives and our leadership to him, our lives will never be the same.

49
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:30,020
Nothing this world has to offer compares to Jesus.

50
00:03:31,660 --> 00:03:39,120
So just for clarity, we did four weeks on prayer, and we're going to do four weeks on fasting, and then we're taking a break for a long period, and we're doing other things.

51
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:42,880
Some people I talked to thought we were going to do like three years straight.

52
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:44,520
That would be ridiculous.

53
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:46,840
I would not put you through that.

54
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:48,140
Four weeks on prayer.

55
00:03:48,140 --> 00:03:50,160
And normally, we're going to do just four weeks at a time.

56
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:56,680
But because prayer and fasting are so regularly connected to one another within the Scriptures, we decided to do eight weeks together.

57
00:03:57,440 --> 00:04:00,640
So, best experience in small groups.

58
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:07,900
There's about 100 or so of us that are doing this in small groups, and so we'd love for you to join with other people as you do this.

59
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:10,680
We're taking a big break because some of this stuff is challenging.

60
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:17,280
Some of this stuff takes time to put into practice, and so you've got time now to work some of these things out in your community.

61
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:20,440
Some of these prayer practice and some of this fasting practice.

62
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:31,000
So, I realize that many of us have little to no experience with fasting, and some of you have already decided that you're going to be taking a three-week sabbatical from church.

63
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,840
Don't do that.

64
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:35,060
The kids' ministry needs help.

65
00:04:35,060 --> 00:04:39,220
So, if you don't want to talk about fasting, don't take a sabbatical from church.

66
00:04:39,220 --> 00:04:39,900
Let me know.

67
00:04:39,900 --> 00:04:45,820
I will get you hooked up with Michelle, and you can hang out with her for a couple of weeks as we finish up this series.

68
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:52,280
But, yeah, fasting has been a practice that is largely ignored and or misunderstood in the modern Western church.

69
00:04:52,280 --> 00:04:56,280
And my hope is that over the next four weeks, we'll clear up some of those questions.

70
00:04:56,280 --> 00:04:57,660
Some of those questions.

71
00:04:57,660 --> 00:05:02,160
I don't promise to answer all of your questions because I am still asking my own.

72
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:11,460
And that we'd cast a compelling vision for why you should consider adding fasting as an intentional spiritual practice in your life.

73
00:05:11,460 --> 00:05:14,260
Now, notice I said spiritual practice.

74
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:24,440
There is a modern, popular idea where fasting is being used all over the country for physical purposes, right?

75
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:34,440
There are scientists and dieticians and all of these people who would make a case for why fasting is actually a good practice for physical health, for dieting and exercise.

76
00:05:34,440 --> 00:05:35,640
And all of those things are good.

77
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:37,100
I'm not saying any of those things are bad.

78
00:05:37,140 --> 00:05:41,900
But the thing that we are talking about is aimed at spiritual practicing.

79
00:05:41,900 --> 00:05:45,980
It's aimed at offering ourselves, our whole selves to God.

80
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:47,900
And so, don't get those two confused.

81
00:05:49,700 --> 00:06:03,100
Also, last thing, I realize that a number of us in this room have certain health concerns and or past or present struggles with body dysmorphia or eating disorders that will likely keep you from these practices.

82
00:06:03,100 --> 00:06:05,000
That is okay.

83
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,360
No shame, no guilt.

84
00:06:08,380 --> 00:06:13,400
And we are going to do our best throughout the next four weeks to offer substitute practices that you can participate in.

85
00:06:13,400 --> 00:06:14,060
Does that sound good?

86
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:14,740
Amen.

87
00:06:14,740 --> 00:06:15,240
Okay.

88
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:17,300
With all those caveats, let me pray.

89
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:27,580
Father, we need you today as much as we needed you yesterday and as much as we're going to need you for the rest of our lives.

90
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:28,680
We need all of you.

91
00:06:28,680 --> 00:06:37,220
We rely ourselves—we throw ourselves at your feet, relying on the power of your Holy Spirit to move in this place,

92
00:06:37,220 --> 00:06:43,780
to bond us together with unity and peace for the sake of the gospel.

93
00:06:43,780 --> 00:06:47,840
God, what the world needs now more than ever is a united church,

94
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:52,920
a united church pursuing you with their whole selves, submitting their lives to your leadership,

95
00:06:52,920 --> 00:06:56,900
so that you might transform us into a person of Christ-like love,

96
00:06:56,900 --> 00:07:04,320
so that we might go into the world making disciples, baptizing them, and teaching them to do what you did.

97
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:14,180
Father, be with us in our time this morning as we address a challenging topic for Western Americans.

98
00:07:14,180 --> 00:07:17,000
May we just hear from you this morning.

99
00:07:18,020 --> 00:07:18,860
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

100
00:07:18,860 --> 00:07:19,600
Amen.

101
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:28,160
I don't know about you, but I often have these experiences where I have an immediate negative reaction to something

102
00:07:28,160 --> 00:07:35,920
that doesn't necessarily make sense or warrant the level of negative reaction that I have.

103
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:39,120
Anybody have any experiences like that?

104
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:43,040
Where you're like, immediate, like, that, no, wrong, bad, awful, terrible.

105
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:47,060
And then you're like, huh, where did that come from?

106
00:07:47,060 --> 00:07:49,780
So, I'll give you an example from my own personal life.

107
00:07:49,780 --> 00:07:52,800
It is very, what I call, Christianese.

108
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:57,060
So, if you're new to the church, this might not make sense to you,

109
00:07:57,060 --> 00:08:00,120
but all of those of you that have been going to church for a long time, I think you'll get it.

110
00:08:00,580 --> 00:08:06,900
For a long time, I had this guttural negative reaction to what the church calls the sinner's prayer.

111
00:08:06,900 --> 00:08:08,960
Anybody familiar with the sinner's prayer?

112
00:08:08,960 --> 00:08:09,900
Help me out a little bit.

113
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:11,200
Okay.

114
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:12,400
That was a decent number of you.

115
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:18,640
The sinner's prayer is this prayer that is offered at the end of a gospel presentation

116
00:08:18,640 --> 00:08:23,160
that you might invite Jesus into your heart, and then because you invite Jesus into your heart,

117
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:24,460
you get to go to heaven with him forever.

118
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:26,480
Everybody good with that?

119
00:08:26,480 --> 00:08:30,000
So, like, I just, for a long time, I just had this really negative,

120
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:31,960
and all of my good Christians in the room are like,

121
00:08:31,960 --> 00:08:34,380
the pastor had a negative reaction to the sinner's prayer.

122
00:08:34,380 --> 00:08:36,260
Just let me, let me finish the illustration.

123
00:08:36,260 --> 00:08:37,440
I think it'll make sense.

124
00:08:37,440 --> 00:08:42,460
And so, for the longest time, I had this self-righteous, like,

125
00:08:42,460 --> 00:08:44,920
indignation towards the sinner's prayer.

126
00:08:44,920 --> 00:08:48,220
And I could give you all the theological reasons for why it wasn't actually in the Bible.

127
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,240
And my arrogant Bible college self could just, like,

128
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:55,680
he could write you a theological treatise on why the sinner's prayer was not good.

129
00:08:55,680 --> 00:09:00,920
However, finally, as I matured in life,

130
00:09:00,920 --> 00:09:07,500
I took a moment to just pause and to consider why it is that I had this negative reaction,

131
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:10,640
which often we don't do when we have immediate negative reactions, right?

132
00:09:10,640 --> 00:09:14,320
We double down, we dig our heels in, and we say,

133
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:17,340
we're right, they're wrong, this reaction is warranted.

134
00:09:17,980 --> 00:09:22,540
But it took me a long time to really consider why I had this negative reaction.

135
00:09:22,540 --> 00:09:28,340
And I realized, finally, that I had seen it misused a few times growing up in my childhood.

136
00:09:28,340 --> 00:09:32,840
That I'd seen speakers at youth rallies literally, like,

137
00:09:32,840 --> 00:09:36,380
tell kids stories about children their age dying.

138
00:09:36,380 --> 00:09:39,400
And they would say, you might die tomorrow.

139
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:42,440
You don't want to go to hell forever, so pray this prayer.

140
00:09:43,840 --> 00:09:44,240
Right?

141
00:09:44,240 --> 00:09:47,040
Anybody ever been a part of anything like that?

142
00:09:47,040 --> 00:09:54,000
So, I realized after a time that, like, I had these negative experiences with this thing,

143
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:57,940
and I had associated all of my negative experiences with that thing.

144
00:09:58,780 --> 00:10:03,120
And I realized over time that there are lovely, godly people who love Jesus,

145
00:10:03,120 --> 00:10:11,060
who present the gospel and ask people to recite the sinner's prayer as a good response to the gospel of Jesus.

146
00:10:11,060 --> 00:10:13,220
I told you this was a Christian illustration.

147
00:10:13,220 --> 00:10:15,040
I'm sorry for those of you that don't know church.

148
00:10:16,440 --> 00:10:21,820
But it took me a while to realize why I was negatively reacting against those things,

149
00:10:21,820 --> 00:10:25,260
and whether or not that negative reaction was actually honoring to God.

150
00:10:25,260 --> 00:10:28,340
And I'm a pastor, so I get to interact with a lot of you.

151
00:10:28,340 --> 00:10:32,220
And I know there are many people that have certain negative reactions to things.

152
00:10:32,220 --> 00:10:35,280
And oftentimes, we just dig our heels in.

153
00:10:35,280 --> 00:10:37,640
Let me give you a couple of examples.

154
00:10:37,640 --> 00:10:42,640
We see something that we disagree with, or that doesn't fit our theological framework,

155
00:10:42,640 --> 00:10:46,800
and we have an immediate negative reaction towards people who think that way.

156
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:51,920
We see a church—now, I'm going to try and—you know how I do both sides?

157
00:10:51,920 --> 00:10:53,700
I think I'm going to try and do both sides.

158
00:10:53,700 --> 00:10:57,780
We see a church of people speaking in tongues, and we're like,

159
00:10:57,780 --> 00:10:59,100
that's not in the Bible.

160
00:10:59,100 --> 00:11:00,020
They shouldn't be doing that.

161
00:11:00,020 --> 00:11:00,560
That's wrong.

162
00:11:00,560 --> 00:11:02,280
Or they're doing it the wrong way, and we get mad.

163
00:11:02,280 --> 00:11:05,920
We get angry, and we're like, no, that can't be—I'm not going to go to church with those people.

164
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:10,780
We see a church with a woman pastor on staff, and we say,

165
00:11:10,780 --> 00:11:11,700
that's not in the Bible.

166
00:11:11,700 --> 00:11:12,460
We can't do that.

167
00:11:12,460 --> 00:11:14,660
They're not allowed to do that, and we dig our heels in.

168
00:11:14,660 --> 00:11:21,500
We see a church baptizing babies, and we say, that's not—am I landing on any yet?

169
00:11:21,500 --> 00:11:25,120
And we have these immediate negative reactions.

170
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:26,220
We dig our heels in.

171
00:11:26,220 --> 00:11:32,580
Rather than pausing, taking those things before the Lord and considering,

172
00:11:32,580 --> 00:11:36,360
is it possible that this reaction isn't warranted?

173
00:11:36,360 --> 00:11:41,400
Is it possible that this reaction doesn't actually honor God or pursue unity in His body?

174
00:11:42,280 --> 00:11:46,700
Is it possible that I might have a misunderstanding with those people?

175
00:11:46,700 --> 00:11:51,760
Have I ever even had a conversation with them or listened to anything that they've said?

176
00:11:53,320 --> 00:12:00,540
Or this one, this one, this one can't be true, but is it possible I'm wrong?

177
00:12:00,540 --> 00:12:03,840
Okay.

178
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:11,200
I bring all of that up because when fasting comes up in spiritual discussions, I find people have an immediate negative reaction.

179
00:12:11,200 --> 00:12:17,940
And I think this morning we should take this opportunity to pause and consider why that might be.

180
00:12:20,520 --> 00:12:25,520
I think we should be able to consider this possibility.

181
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:33,200
Is it possible that we've misunderstood what fasting actually is about, that we've seen it misused or taught in a bad way?

182
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:41,200
Is it possible that we're just unwilling to surrender our food and our comfort to the God who created us and loves us?

183
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:47,920
Is it possible we're stuck in our own self-righteousness, that we're unwilling to consider another perspective?

184
00:12:47,920 --> 00:12:53,380
How might God use these negative reactions to reveal something to us about ourselves?

185
00:12:54,540 --> 00:13:01,920
So, whether you had a negative reaction towards fasting or not, my guess is most of us have a lot of questions about fasting.

186
00:13:01,920 --> 00:13:04,140
Raise your hand if you have a lot of questions about fasting.

187
00:13:04,140 --> 00:13:05,300
Okay.

188
00:13:05,300 --> 00:13:06,260
A number of us.

189
00:13:06,260 --> 00:13:06,600
Thank you.

190
00:13:06,600 --> 00:13:07,160
I'm not alone.

191
00:13:07,160 --> 00:13:07,960
I appreciate that.

192
00:13:07,960 --> 00:13:10,980
What is biblical fasting?

193
00:13:10,980 --> 00:13:14,400
Why should we consider adding it as a practice to our life?

194
00:13:14,400 --> 00:13:19,920
And if, big if, if I consider it, what might be gained from it?

195
00:13:19,920 --> 00:13:24,420
Isn't it funny that that's what we typically ask first as American Christians?

196
00:13:24,660 --> 00:13:25,800
What do I get out of this?

197
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:26,840
Okay.

198
00:13:26,840 --> 00:13:29,980
First off, let me say, I have a lot of questions myself.

199
00:13:29,980 --> 00:13:37,460
I'm relatively new to the practice, and although I found it to be beneficial for me in my relationship with Jesus, I am by no means an expert.

200
00:13:37,460 --> 00:13:44,100
I would ask that you view me as less of a spiritual guide during this series and more of an investigative journalist.

201
00:13:44,100 --> 00:13:48,320
If you're under the age of 30, you might not know what those are.

202
00:13:48,320 --> 00:13:49,600
I don't know if they exist anymore.

203
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:53,240
I'm, see, I'm just taking too many easy.

204
00:13:53,860 --> 00:13:54,820
Low-hanging fruit.

205
00:13:54,820 --> 00:13:56,860
Investigative journalism.

206
00:13:56,860 --> 00:14:01,800
I'm going to ask God and his word questions about this practice and seek to do my best to put forth answers.

207
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:05,360
So, with the rest of our time, we're going to cover three things.

208
00:14:05,360 --> 00:14:09,880
The history of fasting in the Jewish and Christian tradition.

209
00:14:09,880 --> 00:14:12,900
Frequently asked questions about fasting.

210
00:14:12,900 --> 00:14:17,160
And then a basic theology of fasting.

211
00:14:17,160 --> 00:14:18,500
Does that sound like a good plan?

212
00:14:19,140 --> 00:14:22,860
All right, if you have your Bibles, you want to turn to Romans chapter 12, we will get there eventually.

213
00:14:22,860 --> 00:14:25,100
It's going to be a few minutes.

214
00:14:25,100 --> 00:14:27,320
So, history.

215
00:14:27,320 --> 00:14:33,600
Fasting is a practice that is practiced in every major religion.

216
00:14:33,600 --> 00:14:35,920
Judaism, Christianity, Buddhism, Islam.

217
00:14:36,140 --> 00:14:39,420
It's all practiced in every major religion.

218
00:14:39,420 --> 00:14:47,100
However, as far as we can tell, Moses was the first person to practice a fast for spiritual reasons.

219
00:14:47,100 --> 00:14:54,020
As far as we can see in religious texts, we see Moses, who happens to be a hero of both the Jewish faith and the Christian faith.

220
00:14:54,740 --> 00:14:56,560
He does a 40-day fast.

221
00:14:56,560 --> 00:14:58,280
Now, don't worry.

222
00:14:58,280 --> 00:14:59,760
I'm not going to ask you to do that.

223
00:14:59,760 --> 00:15:00,960
40-day fast.

224
00:15:00,960 --> 00:15:03,980
Actually, multiple 40-day fasts for spiritual purposes.

225
00:15:03,980 --> 00:15:12,940
And although there are a ton of examples in the Old Testament and the New Testament of fasting, it's only actually commanded once.

226
00:15:12,940 --> 00:15:15,500
It's commanded of the Jews on the Day of Atonement.

227
00:15:15,500 --> 00:15:18,380
They're supposed to do a 24-hour fast on the Day of Atonement.

228
00:15:18,380 --> 00:15:20,580
So, don't worry.

229
00:15:20,580 --> 00:15:22,460
I'm not going to tell you you have to do it.

230
00:15:22,460 --> 00:15:24,700
Again, this is an invitation for you to consider.

231
00:15:24,700 --> 00:15:32,880
And even though fasting wasn't commanded in the New Testament, Jesus seems to assume that his followers will fast.

232
00:15:32,880 --> 00:15:35,960
In the Sermon on the Mount, he says, when you fast.

233
00:15:35,960 --> 00:15:39,840
Not if you fast, but when you fast.

234
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:44,940
Later, he's being kind of questioned by John the Baptist's disciples.

235
00:15:44,940 --> 00:15:46,540
And they're like, why do your followers not fast?

236
00:15:46,540 --> 00:15:47,040
We fast.

237
00:15:47,040 --> 00:15:47,840
You guys don't fast.

238
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:50,300
He says, well, this is a celebration.

239
00:15:50,300 --> 00:15:51,440
The king is here.

240
00:15:52,040 --> 00:15:54,360
The king is here and he's in the flesh.

241
00:15:54,360 --> 00:15:59,240
But when I leave, when I go, there will be mourning and there will be fasting.

242
00:15:59,240 --> 00:16:04,360
So, Jesus seems to assume that his followers will fast.

243
00:16:04,360 --> 00:16:12,620
By the time of Jesus, the Jews, who had only been commanded to fast once a year, were actually fasting two times a week.

244
00:16:12,620 --> 00:16:16,020
That's pretty zealous, right?

245
00:16:16,020 --> 00:16:27,020
They took a once a year command and they said, we want so desperately to follow God and to restore the righteousness of the Jewish faith that we are going to fast twice a week.

246
00:16:27,020 --> 00:16:27,760
Here's the problem.

247
00:16:29,260 --> 00:16:31,060
They did it for their own self-righteousness.

248
00:16:31,060 --> 00:16:33,340
That's what Jesus is addressing in the Sermon on the Mount.

249
00:16:33,340 --> 00:16:34,140
They did it.

250
00:16:34,140 --> 00:16:37,960
Many of them did it so that they would be seen as holier than the rest of their people.

251
00:16:39,780 --> 00:16:52,340
So, after Jesus died, buried, and rose again, the early church adopted this practice of fasting twice a week.

252
00:16:53,060 --> 00:17:00,920
Many of the early church fathers and mothers would fast on Wednesdays and Fridays, looking at the betrayal of Christ and the crucifixion of Christ.

253
00:17:00,920 --> 00:17:05,700
And throughout the early church, most early church believers did not fast on Sunday.

254
00:17:05,700 --> 00:17:07,320
Sunday is a celebration.

255
00:17:07,320 --> 00:17:09,320
Jesus has rose from the dead.

256
00:17:09,320 --> 00:17:09,980
There's victory.

257
00:17:09,980 --> 00:17:11,500
We feast on Sunday.

258
00:17:11,500 --> 00:17:13,780
So, you get to hear this message on fasting.

259
00:17:13,780 --> 00:17:14,780
You get to leave.

260
00:17:14,780 --> 00:17:16,960
And by God's grace, you get to feast.

261
00:17:16,960 --> 00:17:17,280
Okay?

262
00:17:17,280 --> 00:17:19,120
Sounds good?

263
00:17:19,120 --> 00:17:19,620
All right.

264
00:17:19,620 --> 00:17:20,020
Amen.

265
00:17:21,160 --> 00:17:29,920
Then, later, around 325 A.D., there was a practice by the name of Lent that was adopted by the church around the Council of Nicaea.

266
00:17:29,920 --> 00:17:34,200
Lent actually just started on Wednesday, Valentine's Day.

267
00:17:34,200 --> 00:17:36,340
How many of you are doing any Lenten practices?

268
00:17:36,340 --> 00:17:38,960
A good number of us.

269
00:17:38,960 --> 00:17:46,980
So, Lent is a 40-day, technically 46, but again, we skip Sundays, a 40-day process leading up to Easter,

270
00:17:47,620 --> 00:17:51,620
where fasting is regularly practiced in the church.

271
00:17:51,620 --> 00:17:54,940
The original focus of Lent was for new believers.

272
00:17:54,940 --> 00:17:58,680
It was a 40-day fast in preparation for their baptism.

273
00:17:58,680 --> 00:18:01,680
They would be baptized on Easter Sunday.

274
00:18:01,680 --> 00:18:09,760
So, if you've been baptized here at our church and you didn't fast for 40 days, it doesn't count.

275
00:18:09,760 --> 00:18:10,700
We're going to restart it.

276
00:18:11,920 --> 00:18:12,800
No, I'm just kidding.

277
00:18:12,800 --> 00:18:13,400
I'm just kidding.

278
00:18:13,400 --> 00:18:24,380
But, at a time when persecution in the church was rampant, the church leaders decided they wanted new believers to really count the cost,

279
00:18:24,380 --> 00:18:26,940
to consider what it was that they were signing up for.

280
00:18:26,940 --> 00:18:33,840
Take 40 days to process the invitation of Jesus, which is an invitation of surrender.

281
00:18:34,260 --> 00:18:37,980
Lay down your life, take up your cross, and follow me.

282
00:18:37,980 --> 00:18:42,860
So, the early church invited new believers to participate in Lent.

283
00:18:42,860 --> 00:18:53,060
And to this day, many Christians across the world still practice in certain periods of fasting with their church community or in their individual lives.

284
00:18:53,800 --> 00:18:56,140
All of that to say, my point here is this.

285
00:18:56,140 --> 00:19:02,800
Fasting has played an important role throughout Jewish history, in the life of Jesus, in the early church.

286
00:19:02,800 --> 00:19:09,960
And it continues in the modern church throughout the world, mostly not in the West, but parts of the West.

287
00:19:09,960 --> 00:19:13,320
So, what exactly is fasting?

288
00:19:13,320 --> 00:19:18,560
And what might those of us who fast from it be missing out on?

289
00:19:22,180 --> 00:19:23,060
No, I'm just kidding.

290
00:19:23,060 --> 00:19:26,020
All right.

291
00:19:26,020 --> 00:19:28,580
Frequently asked questions.

292
00:19:28,580 --> 00:19:31,560
What is fasting?

293
00:19:31,560 --> 00:19:34,800
First and foremost, biblical fasting is simple.

294
00:19:34,800 --> 00:19:37,140
It's just not eating for a set period of time.

295
00:19:37,140 --> 00:19:40,480
The Hebrew word literally means to shut one's mouth.

296
00:19:40,480 --> 00:19:42,200
We just talked about Lent.

297
00:19:42,200 --> 00:19:49,080
Modern Lent has kind of come to take on the term fast and applied it to things other than food.

298
00:19:50,040 --> 00:19:51,460
I'm fasting from social media.

299
00:19:51,460 --> 00:19:55,360
I'm fasting from podcasts.

300
00:19:55,360 --> 00:19:57,380
I'm fasting from Netflix.

301
00:19:57,380 --> 00:19:59,220
Whatever it might be.

302
00:19:59,220 --> 00:20:02,840
That is not what biblical fasting means.

303
00:20:02,840 --> 00:20:04,460
That is a practice called abstinence.

304
00:20:04,460 --> 00:20:07,060
Abstinence is an excellent practice.

305
00:20:07,060 --> 00:20:09,820
Modeled in Scripture, we should consider abstinence.

306
00:20:10,820 --> 00:20:17,840
But we shouldn't take the words of the Bible and redefine them and then put them back into the Bible with our new definition.

307
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:24,000
And it doesn't have massive implications for this, but we do this often, right?

308
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:28,680
We redefine biblical terms and we take our meaning of the term and put it back into the text.

309
00:20:28,680 --> 00:20:31,540
And it cannot mean what the text never meant it to mean.

310
00:20:32,520 --> 00:20:35,920
That's just a Bible study principle that you can put in your notes for later.

311
00:20:35,920 --> 00:20:42,320
So, it's also not a restricted diet.

312
00:20:42,320 --> 00:20:44,840
Have we seen the books written about the Daniel fast?

313
00:20:44,840 --> 00:20:45,400
Anybody?

314
00:20:45,400 --> 00:20:47,580
Nobody?

315
00:20:48,260 --> 00:20:48,660
Jason?

316
00:20:48,660 --> 00:20:48,960
All right.

317
00:20:48,960 --> 00:20:49,560
Daniel fast.

318
00:20:49,560 --> 00:20:51,580
Here's the cool thing about the Daniel fast.

319
00:20:51,580 --> 00:20:54,880
Never in the book of Daniel is the word fast used.

320
00:20:54,880 --> 00:20:59,740
So, we just put it in there.

321
00:20:59,740 --> 00:21:02,840
What Daniel did was called a restricted diet.

322
00:21:02,840 --> 00:21:05,120
It is also a helpful practice.

323
00:21:05,120 --> 00:21:06,900
It's just not fasting.

324
00:21:07,340 --> 00:21:09,120
It's a good on-ramp for fasting.

325
00:21:09,120 --> 00:21:14,140
If you've never fasted before, a restricted diet is a great on-ramp for fasting.

326
00:21:14,140 --> 00:21:18,840
Or a substitute for fasting if you have medical issues that won't allow you to fast.

327
00:21:18,840 --> 00:21:22,980
Restricted diet and abstinence are both great substitutes for fasting.

328
00:21:22,980 --> 00:21:26,120
They're just not what the Bible means when the word says fast.

329
00:21:26,120 --> 00:21:28,440
Most fast.

330
00:21:28,440 --> 00:21:28,820
All right.

331
00:21:28,820 --> 00:21:31,960
This is where it's going to get real weird and you guys are going to want to leave for sure.

